<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Mu'min</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">proclaimed caliph c. 1133</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Mu'min was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, proclaimed caliph c. 1133 as successor to the movement's founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>. He led the conquest of Almoravid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, and extended Almohad rule across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He also built the administrative and military institutions that turned a religious movement into a dynastic empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin.jpg" alt="Abd al-Mu'min" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Askelaadden · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdalmumin_ben_ali_nedroma_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His chief disciple <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b> concealed the founder's death for some two years to keep the movement from breaking up, and then emerged as its leader.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1147 <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b>'s forces stormed Marrakesh, killed the last Almoravid ruler, the young Ishaq ibn Ali, and demolished Almoravid monuments, including the palace on whose site the Koutoubia Mosque would rise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Gabès came under Norman influence in the 1140s, and in 1160 the Almohad Caliphate under <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b> conquered it along with the rest of Ifriqiya, ending the Banu Jami dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-jami-emirate-of-gabes">Banu Jami emirate of Gabes</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1159 the Almohad caliph <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b> took Tunis, ending its independence, and the city later became the capital of the Hafsids.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khurasan">Banu Khurasan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1152 the Almohad Caliphate under <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b> took Béjaïa and the Qal'a, and the last emir, Yahya ibn al-Aziz, surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/hammadid-emirate">Hammadid emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almohad caliph <b>Abd al-Mu'min</b> marched east, besieged Mahdia and took it in January 1160, ending Christian rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nedroma, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,-1.75&amp;year=1133" aria-label="Nedroma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4506 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abd al-Mu'min"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4956 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4956 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,-1.75&amp;year=1133">What was here in 1133? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
